     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.10.  The government implemented the initial phase of the transition to the diagnosis related group (DRG) payment method, which include enhancement of the all case rates (ACR), conducting costing studies for DRG, including cost-sharing mechanisms, sensitization of health care providers to DRG, development of an information system for DRG, including groupers, land preparing shadow billing for inpatient health services and climate-sensitive conditions. (PHIC)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2.  To increase sustained financing for UHC, the government (i) implemented the earmarking, on an annual basis, for UHC of the increased excise taxes on alcohol and tobacco products and new excise taxes on heated tobacco and vapor products c and (ii) increased the funds earmarked, on an annual basis, for UHC from the excise taxes on sweetened beverages.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.3.  To strengthen strategic health purchasing, the government, through DOH, DILG, DBM, DOF, and PHIC, established the Special Health Funds which pool local-level funds, and guided their allocation and utilization, including paying the remuneration of additional health workers and incentives for all health workers, majority of whom are women, on a regular basis.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.4.  To expand access to primary care services, the government, through PHIC: (i) made primary care benefit package or Konsulta (including screening for cervical and breast cancers) available to all Filipinos, (ii) expanded the health services and medicines covered by Konsulta, and (iii) included private health care providers as Konsulta providers.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.5.  The government implemented data sharing of UHC-related information systems and databases through data sharing of: (i) Philippine National ID System, (ii) registry of persons with disabilities, (iii) Listahanan 3, or listing of the poor, and (iv) death information records. (PHIC, PSA, DOH, DSWD)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.6.  The government, through the DOH and PHIC, (i) initiated the implementation of the health financing directions in the UHC Act through the Philippine Development Plan 2023&#x2013;2028, the National Health Financing Strategy 2023&#x2013;2028, and the National Objectives for Health 2023&#x2013;2028, and (ii) initiated the review of its financial management which will both ensure their respective accounting systems are aligned with International Public Sector Accounting Standards, Philippine Financial Reporting Standards, and Executive Order No. 29 series of 2023. (DOH, PHIC)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.7.  The government, through DOH, DBM, DOF, PCSO, PAGCOR, and PHIC, implemented the regular and sustained transfer of 50% of the national government share of the gaming income of PAGCOR and 40% of the charity fund of the PCSO, net of documentary stamp tax payments and mandatory contributions, to the national health insurance fund managed by the PHIC to finance UHC implementation, with &#x20b1;21 billion appropriated to PHIC through the 2023 General Appropriations Act. (DOH, DBM, PSCO, PAGCOR, PHIC)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.8.  The government, through DOH, implemented the Philippine Medicines Policy 2022-2028, which provided overall policy guidance and direction to all stakeholders, including those in the private sector, to improve access to essential medicines, including: (i) issuing guidelines on the Cancer Supportive and Palliative Medicines Access Program, which increased access to medicines for high-burden and high mortality cancers, (ii) expanding PHIC &#x201c;Z-benefit&#x201d; packages to cover more essential medicines, and (iii) issuing an Integrated Roadmap of the Philippine Pharmaceutical Industry to increase the availability of medicines by strengthening local production. (DOH)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.9.  The government, through PHIC, initiated the expansion of the Konsulta programi with existing benefits responding to the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) (benefit packages addressing animal bites, HIV-AIDS, malaria, and tuberculosis) into capitation-paid Konsulta+SDG benefits, and implemented contracting with primary care provider networks. (PHIC)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.1.  To increase access to climate-smart, disaster-resilient, environmentally sustainable, and gender sensitive health facilities, the government through DOH (i) implemented the Philippine Health Facility Development Plan and (ii) rolled out the updated Health Care Waste Management Manual.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2.2.  The government, through DOH, implemented the Green and Safe Health Facilities system, which promotes incorporated health sector gender and development (GAD) agenda and gender mainstreaming in the development planning process, was aligned with the World Health Resolution on Climate Change and Health, and the Geneva Pledge on Climate Change Action, and recognizes 33 hospitals as green and safe health facilities. (DOH)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.1.  To accelerate making health information systems interoperable, the government, through DOH and PHIC, mandated compliance of government and private entities with health information systems with (i) the National Health Data Standards for interoperability ae and integrated health information system, and (ii) requirements for submission of health and health-related data.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.2.  To improve gender parity and the technical expertise of the PHIC Board governing the performance of PHIC, the government added technical expertise with 3 additional board members with expertise in public health, management, finance, and health economics, and increased gender parity by requiring at least 1 of the 3 experts, and at least 2 of the 5 current sectoral board members to be female.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.3.  To ensure the systematic identification of the potential negative and positive health impacts of development projects, the DOH and DILG operationalized health impact assessments of development projects.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.4.  The government, (i) through DOH, DICT, and PHIC, required all government and private entities with health information systems to conform to updated DOH and PHIC National Health Data Standards for interoperability and to comply with an interoperability validation conducted by DICT, and (ii) through PHIC and DOH established a single point of submission and authority for unified data collection for UHC-related information systems and databases, including the collection of climate sensitive diseases, through the National Health Data Repository Framework. (DOH, DICT, PHIC)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.5.  The government, through the DOH and PSA, (i) made all health, nutrition, and demographic-related administrative and survey studies accessible to the public, (ii) conducted burden of diseases estimates and health expenditure studies including those diseases associated with climate change, and (iii) completed Phase 1 of the UHC Annual Survey, including the health impacts of climate change and gender-related indicators. (DOH, PSA)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.6.  The government, (i) through the Council of Good Local Governance, included the Seal of Good Local Governance (SGLG) metrics on health compliance and responsiveness, disaster preparedness, and social protection, including the vulnerable and marginalized, and awarded 352 local government units (LGUs) in 2022 allowing those LGUs access to the LGU Incentive Fund, and (ii) through DOH, implemented the Healthy Pilipinas awards for healthy communities with 31 LGUs awarded in 2022. (DOH, DILG)</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
